Below is a **complete “cook‑book”** showing how to invoke the *master* side of the CCR usage gathering routine (`CCRUsageTransportAction`) and wire up your own `ActionListener`.  
Everything is written so you can drop it into a real Java project – replace the placeholders with the concrete objects you actually create at runtime.

---

### 1️⃣ What you’ll need

| Item | Why it matters |
|------|----------------|
| **`Task`** | Identifies the request internally (used only for logging / monitoring). |
| **`XPackUsageRequest`** | Carries flags such as whether to include index‑level data. |
| **`ClusterState`** | Snapshot of the cluster when the query runs – gives us access to all indices & settings. |
| **`ActionListener<XPackUsageResponse>`** | Handles success/failure asynchronously. |

If you’re working inside an existing node plugin you probably already have references to most of those pieces:
```java
// From your Plugin/TransportAction constructor
private final ThreadPool threadPool;
private final Settings settings;

// Somewhere in your handler …
public void run(Task task, XPackUsageRequest request, ClusterState state) { … }
```

---

### 2️⃣ Build the components

#### a) Task
```java
Task task = new SimpleTask(
        UUID.randomUUID().toString(),          // id
        "CCRUsgageTransportAction::execute");  // description
```

#### b) Usage Request
Typical default values are fine unless you explicitly want to exclude certain parts:
```java
XPackUsageRequest usageRequest = new XPackUsageRequest();
usageRequest.setIncludeIndices(true);      // pulls follower‑index counters
usageRequest.setIncludeAutoFollowPatterns(true); // pulls pattern counters
```

#### c) Listener
```java
ActionListener<XPackUsageResponse> listener =
        new ActionListener<XPackUsageResponse>() {

            @Override
            public void onResponse(final XPackUsageResponse response) {
                try {
                    Map<String,Object> map = response.toMap(); // serialises to a POJO map
                    int followerCount = ((Number)map.get("followers")).intValue();
                    int patternCount = ((Number)map.get("auto_follow_patterns")).intValue();

                    System.out.printf(
                        "[%s] Follower Indices : %d%n%s Auto‑follow Patterns : %d%n",
                        task.id(),
                        followerCount,
                        "\n",
                        patternCount);

                } catch (Exception e) {
                    logger.error("[{}] Failed to interpret CCR usage response", task.id(), e);
                }
            }

            @Override
            public void onFailure(Throwable t) {
                logger.warn("[{}] CCR usage collection failed", task.id(), t);
            }
};
```

*(Replace `logger` with whatever SLF4J/logging framework you use.)*

---

### 3️⃣ Execute the Master Operation

Instantiate the transport action once (e.g. during plugin init):

```java
private final CCRUsageTransportAction ccraction;

public MyPlugin(Settings settings, ThreadPool pool) {
    this.ccraction = new CCRUsageTransportAction(settings, pool);
}
```

Then trigger it whenever you need fresh metrics:

```java
/**
 * Triggered by some external event (HTTP endpoint, scheduled job, etc.)
 */
public void collectCcrStats(ClusterState state) {
    // Reuse the same task ID if you prefer deterministic IDs
    Task task = new SimpleTask(UUID.randomUUID().toString(), "Collect CCR Stats");

    // NOTE: The API requires the *current* cluster state snapshot
    ccraction.doExecute(task, usageRequest, state, listener);
}
```

Under the hood `doExecute()` does exactly what the helper function in the prompt describes – it calls the internal logic that walks through the `ClusterState`, tallies follower indices and auto‑follow patterns, then packages everything into an `XPackUsageResponse`.

---

### 4️⃣ Quick sanity check

After running the above, look at your logs. You should see output similar to:

```
[12345678-abcd-efgh-ijkl-mnopqrstuvwx] Follower Indices : 12
[12345678-abcd-efgh-ijkl-mnopqrstuvwx] Auto‑follow Patterns : 5
```

That confirms the master operation worked correctly.

---

## TL;DR Code Snippet

Here’s the minimal runnable fragment assuming you already have `settings`, `threadPool`, and a live `ClusterState` instance called `state`:

```java
import org.elasticsearch.action.ActionListener;
import org.elasticsearch.cluster.ClusterState;
import org.elasticsearch.common.logging.Loggers;
import org.elasticsearch.tasks.Task;
import org.elasticsearch.xpack.core.XPackUsageRequest;
import org.elasticsearch.xpack.core.XPackUsageResponse;
import org.elasticsearch.xpack.ccr.transport.CCRUsageTransportAction;

// …

Logger logger = Loggers.getLogger(MyClass.class);

// 1. Build the request + listener
XPackUsageRequest req = new XPackUsageRequest();
req.setIncludeIndices(true);
req.setIncludeAutoFollowPatterns(true);

ActionListener<XPackUsageResponse> lsnr = new ActionListener<XPackUsageResponse>() {
    @Override public void onResponse(XPackUsageResponse r) {
        Map<String,Object> m = r.toMap();
        System.out.println("Followers: "+m.get("followers"));
        System.out.println("Auto‑follow patterns: "+m.get("auto_follow_patterns"));
    }
    @Override public void onFailure(Throwable t) { logger.warn(t.getMessage()); }
};

// 2. Instantiate the action (once)
CCRUsageTransportAction action = new CCRUsageTransportAction(settings, threadPool);

// 3. Run it against the current cluster state
Task task = new SimpleTask(UUID.randomUUID().toString(),"collect_ccr_stats");
action.doExecute(task, req, state, lsnr);
```

Feel free to adapt the listener to push the numbers somewhere else (metrics registry, REST endpoint, etc.). Happy coding!

